(go/type Marshaler "Marshaler is implemented by types that can marshal themselves.\nIt is recommended that types implement [MarshalerTo] unless the implementation\nis trying to avoid directly depending on the \"jsontext\" package.\n\nImplementations should return a buffer that is safe\nfor the caller to retain and potentially mutate.\n\nImplementations must not return [errors.ErrUnsupported].\n\nIf the returned error is a [SemanticError], then unpopulated fields\nof the error may be populated by [json] with additional context.\nErrors of other types are wrapped within a [SemanticError].\n\nImplementations should assume [Deterministic] is true and return\ndeterministic output.\n" (interface
    (MarshalJSON [] :results [(slice byte) error])))











































(go/type MarshalerTo "MarshalerTo is implemented by types that can marshal themselves.\nIt is recommended that types implement MarshalerTo instead of [Marshaler]\nsince it is both more performant and more flexible.\nIf a type implements both Marshaler and MarshalerTo,\nthen MarshalerTo takes precedence. In such a case, both implementations\nshould aim to have equivalent behavior for the default marshal options.\n\nThe implementation must write only one JSON value to the Encoder.\nAlternatively, it may return [errors.ErrUnsupported] without mutating\nthe Encoder. The \"json\" package calling the method will\nuse the next available JSON representation for the receiver type,\nas described in [Marshal].\nImplementations must not retain the pointer to [jsontext.Encoder].\n\nIf the returned error is a [SemanticError], then unpopulated fields\nof the error may be populated by [json] with additional context.\nErrors of other types are wrapped within a [SemanticError],\nexcept for IO errors.\n\nThe MarshalJSONTo method should not be called directly as it may\nreturn sentinel errors that need special handling.\nUsers should instead call [MarshalEncode], which handles such cases.\n\nImplementations should inspect the marshal options from\n[jsontext.Encoder.Options] and adjust behavior to respect the options as\nnecessary.\n\nThe following options may be relevant to MarshalerTo implementations:\n\n- [Deterministic]: if the implementation may produce non-deterministic output\n- [StringifyNumbers]: if the type is represented as a JSON number\n\nSeveral options, such as [FormatNilSliceAsNull], apply only to native Go\ntypes. Thus, these options are typically not directly relevant to\nMarshalerTo implementations. However, types representing a composite type\nshould marshal contained types using [MarshalEncode] to ensure these options\napply to the contained types. Similarly, [WithMarshalers] may influence\nmarshaling of any contained type within a composite type.\n\nAll other options are automatically handled outside of the MarshalerTo\nimplementation, and thus are not relevant to implementations.\n" (interface
    (MarshalJSONTo ^error [(* jsontext/Encoder)])))


















(go/type Unmarshaler "Unmarshaler is implemented by types that can unmarshal themselves.\nIt is recommended that types implement [UnmarshalerFrom] unless the implementation\nis trying to avoid a direct dependency on the \"jsontext\" package.\n\nThe input can be assumed to be a valid encoding of a JSON value\nif called from unmarshal functionality in this package.\nIt is recommended that UnmarshalJSON implement merge semantics\nwhen unmarshaling into a pre-populated value, as described in [Unmarshal].\n\nImplementations must not retain or mutate the input []byte.\n\nImplementations must not return [errors.ErrUnsupported].\n\nIf the returned error is a [SemanticError], then unpopulated fields\nof the error may be populated by [json] with additional context.\nErrors of other types are wrapped within a [SemanticError].\n" (interface
    (UnmarshalJSON ^error [(slice byte)])))











































(go/type UnmarshalerFrom "UnmarshalerFrom is implemented by types that can unmarshal themselves.\nIt is recommended that types implement UnmarshalerFrom instead of [Unmarshaler]\nsince this is both more performant and more flexible.\nIf a type implements both Unmarshaler and UnmarshalerFrom,\nthen UnmarshalerFrom takes precedence. In such a case, both implementations\nshould aim to have equivalent behavior for the default unmarshal options.\n\nThe implementation must read only one JSON value from the Decoder.\nIt is recommended that UnmarshalJSONFrom implement merge semantics when\nunmarshaling into a pre-populated value, as described in [Unmarshal].\nAlternatively, it may return [errors.ErrUnsupported] without mutating\nthe Decoder. The \"json\" package calling the method will\nuse the next available JSON representation for the receiver type.\nImplementations must not retain the pointer to [jsontext.Decoder].\n\nIf the returned error is a [SemanticError], then unpopulated fields\nof the error may be populated by [json] with additional context.\nErrors of other types are wrapped within a [SemanticError],\nexcept for [jsontext.SyntacticError]s and IO errors.\n\nThe UnmarshalJSONFrom method should not be called directly as it may\nreturn sentinel errors that need special handling.\nUsers should instead call [UnmarshalDecode], which handles such cases.\n\nImplementations should inspect the unmarshal options from\n[jsontext.Decoder.Options] and adjust behavior to respect the options as\nnecessary.\n\nThe following options may be relevant to UnmarshalerFrom implementations:\n\n- [StringifyNumbers]: if the type is represented as a JSON number\n\nSeveral options, such as [FormatNilSliceAsNull], apply only to native Go\ntypes. Thus, these options are typically not directly relevant to\nUnmarshalerFrom implementations. However, types representing a composite\ntype should unmarshal contained types using [UnmarshalDecode] to ensure\nthese options apply to the contained types. Similarly, [WithUnmarshalers]\nmay influence unmarshaling of any contained type within a composite type.\n\nAll other options are automatically handled outside of the UnmarshalerFrom\nimplementation, and thus are not relevant to implementations.\n" (interface
    (UnmarshalJSONFrom ^error [(* jsontext/Decoder)])))


